<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 83 0 R
/T (A sorted array supports binary search in O\(log n\) and listing all keys in O\(n\). Insertion into the middle requires shifting elements and is O\(n\). A BST insertion costs O\(h\), which is O\(log n\) when balanced but O\(n\) in the worst case. A BST node stores two child pointers, adding pointer overhead per key; an array stores keys contiguously and is generally more cache-friendly. A sorted array is attractive when updates are rare and searches are frequent.)
/E (A sorted array supports binary search in O\(log n\) and listing all keys in O\(n\). Insertion into the middle requires shifting elements and is O\(n\). A BST insertion costs O\(h\), which is O\(log n\) when balanced but O\(n\) in the worst case. A BST node stores two child pointers, adding pointer overhead per key; an array stores keys contiguously and is generally more cache-friendly. A sorted array is attractive when updates are rare and searches are frequent.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [32]
>>
