/T (A binary min-heap also finds the smallest element in O\(1\) at the root and inserts in O\(log n\). However, locating and removing an arbitrary process requires O\(n\) search to find its array index, followed by O\(log n\) repair. A red-black tree supports search, insert and delete in O\(log n\). A heap is compact in an array; a tree node needs key, colour and child/parent pointers.)
/E (A binary min-heap also finds the smallest element in O\(1\) at the root and inserts in O\(log n\). However, locating and removing an arbitrary process requires O\(n\) search to find its array index, followed by O\(log n\) repair. A red-black tree supports search, insert and delete in O\(log n\). A heap is compact in an array; a tree node needs key, colour and child/parent pointers.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
