/Title (A B-tree is preferred for databases and file systems because it minimizes disk I/O by matching node size to disk blocks. AVL trees are excellent for in-memory data, where pointer traversal is cheap and a tighter balance can improve lookup time. An AVL tree with one node per disk block may need roughly 23 reads, compared with about 4 for an order-100 B-tree, and can waste most of each block because it stores only one key per node.)
